 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery.** Committed revisions are pushed to browsers over WebSocket connections the board operates itself. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected: it meets the freshness target but leaves every browser a few seconds behind while still requiring cursor queries in the store and a polling loop in every browser. A hosted realtime service was rejected: recurring fees and provider coupling for a single-purpose board. Self-hosting accepts connection-capacity and reconnect operations work in exchange for fast updates with no vendor dependency. Full tradeoff analysis: `docs/decisions/delivery-approach.md`.
